# How a NOT COVERED is triaged (the gate above only looks at LIVED, so this does
# not block: it is background work).
#
# gremlins' NOT COVERED is NOT a verdict, it is a suspicion, for two reasons that
# can be measured and need not be believed:
#
# 1. Broken coverage mapping. gremlins looks for a block that STARTS on the
#    mutant's line; Go's coverage is emitted per block that SPANS a range
#    (line.col, line.col). A mutant in column 22 of a line whose block starts at
#    54 is inside that block and still comes out NOT COVERED. With our own profile
#    (go test -covermode=count -coverpkg=./... -coverprofile) and looking for the
#    block that CONTAINS the position, the 912 KILLED mutants of a run all fall
#    inside a block with count > 0: all 912, no failures. That is the control that
#    makes the method reliable.
#
# 2. `-coverpkg=./...` is mandatory with this layout. internal/testutil is used by
#    other packages' tests, and without -coverpkg the profile only measures the
#    package under test: its 14 NOT COVERED were live callers. With -coverpkg they
#    become an ARTEFACT.
#
# Breakdown of a full run (180 NOT COVERED): 48 mapping artefacts, 114 lines with
# no instrumentable block (const, signatures, comments: the mutants are not
# executable code and the `+` ones between strings do not even compile when
# mutated), 18 real gaps, of which 11 are not viable, 2 are main() code with no
# tests, 3 are identities (this section + the app.go one), and 2 were real work:
# app.go:454 (the discovery.Scan error never reached the user) and update.go:60
# (the cache was never saved). Both closed with a test.